  "why": "The statement names two conjectures (Arnold and Arnold–Givental), each with several standard forms, so no single-form result is whole. Pre-snapshot work settles the homological Arnold form over Q, F_p and Z and many Lagrangian classes, all as cases; Ma 2008's general claim is set aside as not credible (withdrawn version, withdrawn companions, no uptake in 18 years, ignored by later work). After the snapshot: BSWX 2026 proves the transverse Arnold–Givental bound for real loci over F2 — Givental's original formulation is about such loci, but the statement also names the Arnold conjecture, so it is a case regardless of the real-locus question. Asselle–Starostka, Gong 2025 and Jiao give degenerate Hamiltonian cases (the latter two doubtful for unclear scope). Gong 2026 refutes a degenerate homological Lagrangian bound, which is a strengthening of the transverse Arnold–Givental statement rather than the statement itself, so variant; its AI disclosure names no system and says nothing of what was produced (systems empty), so none. The OpenAI family refutes the strong (Morse-number) Arnold form on explicit manifolds and the degenerate cuplength/critical-number form on Q³; a counterexample settles that named form negatively, so case/refuted/counterexample, generated by the README's account.",
